</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS2.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Operational costs: latency and token usage</h5>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS2.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS2.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The improved performance of KBA is associated with increased resource consumption (Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Operational costs: latency and token usage ‣ 4.4.2 Comparative Performance Analysis ‣ 4.4 Results and Analysis ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Knowledge Base-Aware Orchestration: A Dynamic, Privacy-Preserving Method for Multi-Agent Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). The dynamic knowledge probing step introduces higher token usage and longer execution times compared to the description-driven baseline. However, this overhead primarily reflects the absence of caching in our experimental setup. As discussed in Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Methodology ‣ Knowledge Base-Aware Orchestration: A Dynamic, Privacy-Preserving Method for Multi-Agent Systems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, the introduction of a semantic cache is expected to substantially mitigate these costs in practice, especially under workloads with recurring queries.</p>
</div>
